"VideoWorld 2: Learning Transferable Knowledge from Real-world Videos (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.10102v1)\nciteturn31705view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31698view2\",\"lineno\":382}); Total lines: 527\nL356:   * Yu et al. [2025a] Jiwen Yu, Yiran Qin, Haoxuan Che, Quande Liu, Xintao Wang, Pengfei Wan, Di Zhang, Kun Gai, Hao Chen, and Xihui Liu. A survey of interactive generative video. arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.21853, 2025a.\nL357:   * Yu et al. [2025b] Jiwen Yu, Yiran Qin, Xintao Wang, Pengfei Wan, Di Zhang, and Xihui Liu. Gamefactory: Creating new games with generative interactive videos. arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.08325, 2025b.\nL358:   * Yu et al. [2024] Lijun Yu, Jose Lezama, Nitesh Bharadwaj Gundavarapu, Luca Versari, Kihyuk Sohn, David Minnen, Yong Cheng, Agrim Gupta, Xiuye Gu, Alexander G Hauptmann, et al. Language model beats diffusion-tokenizer is key to visual generation. In ICLR, 2024.\nL359:   * Yuan et al. [2025] Hangjie Yuan, Weihua Chen, Jun Cen, Hu Yu, Jingyun Liang, Shuning Chang, Zhihui Lin, Tao Feng, Pengwei Liu, Jiazheng Xing, et al. Lumos-1: On autoregressive video generation from a unified model perspective. arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.08801, 2025.\nL360:   * Zhang et al. [2023] Lvmin Zhang, Anyi Rao, and Maneesh Agrawala. Adding conditional control to text-to-image diffusion models. In CVPR, pages 3836–3847, 2023.\nL361:   * Zhang et al. [2018] Richard Zhang, Phillip Isola, Alexei A Efros, Eli Shechtman, and Oliver Wang. The unreasonable effectiveness of deep features as a perceptual metric. In CVPR, pages 586–595, 2018.\nL362:   * Zhao et al. [2025] Long Zhao, Sanghyun Woo, Ziyu Wan, Yandong Li, Han Zhang, Boqing Gong, Hartwig Adam, Xuhui Jia, and Ting Liu. Epsilon-vae: Denoising as visual decoding. arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.04081, 2025.\nL363:   * Zhao et al. [2023] Wayne Xin Zhao, Kun Zhou, Junyi Li, Tianyi Tang, Xiaolei Wang, Yupeng Hou, Yingqian Min, Beichen Zhang, Junjie Zhang, Zican Dong, et al. A survey of large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.18223, 1(2), 2023.\nL364:   * Zheng et al. [2025] Ruijie Zheng, Jing Wang, Scott Reed, Johan Bjorck, Yu Fang, Fengyuan Hu, Joel Jang, Kaushil Kundalia, Zongyu Lin, Loic Magne, et al. Flare: Robot learning with implicit world modeling. arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.15659, 2025.\nL365:   * Zhou et al. [2024] Gaoyue Zhou, Hengkai Pan, Yann LeCun, and Lerrel Pinto. Dino-wm: World models on pre-trained visual features enable zero-shot planning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2411.04983, 2024.\nL366:   * Zhu et al. [2024] Zheng Zhu, Xiaofeng Wang, Wangbo Zhao, Chen Min, Nianchen Deng, Min Dou, Yuqi Wang, Botian Shi, Kai Wang, Chi Zhang, et al. Is sora a world simulator? a comprehensive survey on general world models and beyond. arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.03520, 2024.\nL367: \\beginappendix\nL368: \nL369: In this supplementary material, we provide additional details and analyses:\nL370: \nL371:   * •\nL372: \nL373: Sec. cite19†8 describes the training setup, the structure of the dLDM, and other implementation details.\nL374: \nL375:   * •\nL376: \nL377: Sec. cite20†9 provides further analysis of Video-CraftBench.\nL378: \nL379:   * •\nL380: \nL381: Sec. cite21†10 provides additional visualizations of both our method and the baselines.\nL382: ## 8 Implementation Details\nL383: \nL384: Training details. We present the detailed training configurations of dynamics-enhanced latent dynamics model and auto-regressive transformer in Tab. cite127†4 .\nL385: Dynamics-enhanced latent dynamics model (dLDM). As shown in Fig. 4, the proposed dLDM consists of four components. (i) A causal encoder extracts visual features. (ii) A set of learnable queries attends to the encoder features to extract visual changes and produce the latent dynamic codes. (iii) A decoder reconstructs frames from the latent dynamic codes and the feature of the first frame, providing coarse motion cues.\nL386: (iv) A pretrained Video Diffusion Model (VDM) generates high-fidelity future frames by taking three inputs: the first frame, the low-resolution decoder outputs, and the latent dynamic codes. The latent codes are injected into the VDM via causal cross-attention, allowing the codes to concentrate on task-relevant dynamics while the VDM handles visual appearance modeling. We provide PyTorch-style pseudocode for the full dLDM and its components in Alg. LABEL:alg:code.\nL387: Latent pre-training on CALVIN. In Sec. 5.4 (line 432), we present a latent pre-training experiment where latent dynamic tokens serve as prediction targets. This enables the model to learn manipulation knowledge from unlabeled data, thereby facilitating better adaptation to ground-truth actions. Following LAPA [cite88†72 ]’s protocol, given a task trajectory $x_{0:T}$, we train the AR Transformer to predict the quantized latent dynamic embeddings $\\{z_{k}^{n}\\}^{K,N}_{k=1,n=1}$ (defined in Sec.\nL388: 3 line 198), conditioned on the language instruction and the first image $x_{0}$. We extend the AR transformer’s vocabulary to include these latent dynamic tokens. Since this pre-training does not rely on ground-truth actions, it can use any unlabeled video data.\nL389: Following latent pre-training, we further fine-tune the model with ground-truth labels to derive actions executable in the simulation environment. We append a new action head (an MLP layer) to the transformer to map the hidden states, which are originally used for latent token prediction, to real actions via an $\\ell_{2}$ loss.\nL390: To ensure compatibility with cross-environment data, we exclusively use CALVIN’s static camera images during both pre-training and fine-tuning, omitting wrist images and state parameters. This latent pre-training should provide the AR transformer with a manipulation prior, leading to better fine-tuning performance.\nL391: ## 9 Details in Video-CraftBench\nL392: \nL393: Key steps for evaluation. In Sec. 4.2 (line 314), we define seven key steps in the paper folding task to evaluate the task success rate. Fig. cite108†6 (top) provides a schematic diagram of these key steps.\nL394: Train and test environments. Fig. cite108†6 (bottom) illustrates the Video-CraftBench training and testing environments. To evaluate the model’s generalization to new environments, the test set features diverse variations compared to the training set. Specifically, the paper folding test set differs in background, texture, paper appearance, and camera viewpoints, while the block-building varies in initial block arrangement, color combinations, and camera angles.\nL395: Config  | dLDM  | AR Transformer\nL396: optimizer  | AdamW  | AdamW\nL397: base learning rate  | 1e-4  | 3e-4\nL398: weight decay  | 0.1  | 0.05\nL399: optimizer monmentum  | $\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$=0.9,0.99  | $\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$=0.9,0.98\nL400: batch size  | 128  | 256\nL401: learning rate schedule  | WarmipDecayLR  | WarmipDecayLR\nL402: warmup iterations  | 2e+3  | 5e+3\nL403: max iterations  | 1e+5  | 5e+4\nL404: augmentations  | None  | None\nL405: Training Loss  | L2 & Denoising loss  | CE loss\nL406: Training Target  | Recon. & Denoising  | Next token pred.\nL407: Table 4: Training configurations for the dynamics-enhanced latent dynamics model and auto-regressive transformer.\nL408: Task analysis. Fig. cite128†10 shows the task duration distribution of Video-CraftBench. The largest portion (37.3%) falls within 45–60 seconds, followed by 27.1% at 60–90 seconds, while short tasks (20–30s) make up only 10.9%. This distribution highlights our emphasis on long-horizon tasks. Regarding task types (Fig. cite128†10 ), paper folding accounts for 55.3% (5.2 hours) of the total duration due to its longer execution time, while block building comprises 1.8 hours.\nL409: Task classifier of Video-CraftBench. We detail the training of the classifier used to evaluate key step completion in Sec. 4.2 (line 314). Its primary objective is to determine whether the model has reached key steps by analyzing paper shapes and block arrangements. Appearance distractions, such as texture, color, and the background drift shown in Fig. cite98†8 (last row), are disregarded. These visual quality is assessed separately via LPIPS and SSIM.\nL410: Therefore, we initialize the classifier with DINOv2-Base (86M parameters), chosen for its exceptional geometric awareness and ability to extract fine-grained features.\nL411: To train this classifier, we construct a dataset of $\\sim$25k labeled frames. First, we extract $\\sim$15k frames of both key and non-key steps from the training and testing environments. To further enhance diversity and mitigate bias, we generate task execution videos using all models involved in the evaluation, manually verify successful trajectories, and extract an additional $\\sim$10k frames. We then attach a classification head to the DINOv2 backbone and fine-tune the entire model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards Poisoning Robustness Certification for Natural Language Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.09757v1)\nciteturn31705view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31698view0\",\"lineno\":382}); Total lines: 489\nL367:   * Zou et al. (2023) A. Zou, Z. Wang, N. Carlini, M. Nasr, J. Z. Kolter, and M. Fredrikson Universal and transferable adversarial attacks on aligned language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.15043. Cited by: cite110†§5.1 .\nL368: ## Appendix A Multi-Sample Stability\nL369: A framework for computing a multi-sample certificate for a group of points in general classification can be extended to individual-token prediction by framing the latter as a multi-class classification problem. Once a guarantee is obtained for every token position in a sentence with respect to a group of points, it is possible to compute an expectation-based version of the robustness horizon in Definition cite111†4.4 as a proxy for stability.\nL370: We thus provide below a collective certification technique for the deep partition aggregation (DPA) method, which we leverage extensively in our experiments in Section §cite19†5.3 .\nL371: ###### Theorem A.1 (Collective/Multi-sample certificates for DPA).\nL372: Let $\\{x^{(i)}\\}_{i=1}^{N}$ denote a collection of $N$ test data points, $K$ be a global poisoning budget, and $\\{\\mathcal{P}_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{M}$ be disjoint shards with associated base classifiers $f_{j}$.\nL373: For each input $x^{(i)}$, let $c_{1}^{(i)}$ and $c_{2}^{(i)}$ denote the top two classes with the associated number of votes $v_{1}^{(i)}$ and, respectively, $v_{2}^{(i)}$, and let $\\tau_{i}=v_{1}^{(i)}-v_{2}^{(i)}+\\mathbb{I}(c_{2}^{(i)}>c_{1}^{(i)})$ be the aggregation margin required to flip the ensemble prediction.\nL374: The collective robustness certificate $\\mathcal{A}(K)$ is provided in the form of the worst-case performance against an adversary with a global perturbation budget $K$, defined as the minimum fraction of the $N$ test points that remain correctly classified:\nL375:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}(K)=$  | $\\displaystyle\\max_{a}\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\mathbb{I}\\bigg[\\tau_{i}\\leq\\sum_{j=1}^{M}\\psi_{j}^{(i)}\\bigg]\\quad\\text{s.t.}$  |\nL376:  | $\\displaystyle\\psi_{j}^{(i)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{I}\\{a_{j}\\geq R_{j}(x^{(i)})\\}\\left(1+\\mathbb{I}\\{f_{j}(x^{(i)})=c_{1}^{(i)}\\}\\right)$  |\nL377:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^{T}a_{j}$  | $\\displaystyle=K$  |\nL378:  | $\\displaystyle R_{j}(x^{(i)})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}K+1&\\text{if }f_{j}(x^{(i)})\\neq c_{2}^{(i)}\\\\\nL379: 1&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\\>,\\forall j$  |\nL380: Proof. To flip a prediction for $x^{(i)}$ using the minimum number of manipulations, an adversary must bridge the gap $\\tau_{i}$ by making the shards vote for a target class $c_{2}^{(i)}$.\nL381: The formulation accounts for three cases: shards already voting for $c_{2}^{(i)}$ cannot be further exploited ($R_{j}=K+1$), while changing a shard’s prediction from $c_{1}^{(i)}$ to $c_{2}^{(i)}$ reduces the margin by two votes and from any other class by one (since $\\mathbb{I}\\{f_{j}(x^{(i)})=c_{1}^{(i)}\\}=0$ in that scenario). ∎\nL382: ## Appendix B Proofs\nL383: ###### Theorem cite112†4.3 .\nL384: \nL385: The value $r_{t}$ computed by Algorithm cite71†1 is a sound lower bound on the $i^{th}$-token validity radius. For any global perturbation budget $K\\leq r_{\\text{target}}$, the adversary is guaranteed to fail in making the target token $t$ the majority prediction.\nL386: \nL387: Proof. Firstly, we restate the lower bound on the $i^{\\text{th}}$-token validity radius:\nL388: \nL389:  | $$r_{t}=\\Phi[s^{*}-1]+\\left\\lfloor\\dfrac{(V_{c}[s^{*}]-\\Phi[s^{*}-1]+1)\\cdot s^{*}}{s^{*}+1}\\right\\rfloor.$$  |\nL390: Secondly, we note that in Algorithm cite71†1 , $\\Delta[j]$ captures the votes released at phase $j$, $\\Phi[s]$ is the cumulative vote count of target class $t$, and $s^{*}$ is the first phase at which $t$ strictly surpasses all remaining classes.\nL391: The target class $t$ starts with $v_{t}$ votes. An adversary seeks to minimally modify the ensemble so that $t$ becomes the predicted class. The worst case for the defender corresponds to the optimal adversary’s strategy, which works by redistributing votes from competing classes to $t$ in order to maximize the total number of votes transferred before the leading competing classes are overtaken.\nL392: This strategy follows a cascading reduction: the adversary first lowers the top class to match the second, then reduces both together to match the third, and so on. In phase $j$, the top $j$ classes are equalized and jointly reduced to the next vote level, with all removed votes reassigned to $t$. Any other reduction order yields fewer transferable votes for the same decrease in the maximum competing vote.\nL393: Therefore, $r_{t}$ is a strict lower bound because at each phase, essentially every vote is used at its maximum “capacity” or “harmfulness” by the attacker, by always redistributing it from the top class to the target $t$. ∎\nL394: ###### Theorem cite85†4.8 .\nL395: \nL396: We define $\\mathcal{A}(K)$ as the solution to the MILP in Definition cite73†4.7 . This value serves as a collective robustness certificate,providing a sound lower bound on accuracy under a global perturbation budget $K$. Consequently, for any attack within this budget, at least $\\lfloor N\\cdot\\mathcal{A}(K)\\rfloor$ samples are guaranteed to maintain their original predictions.\nL397: \nL398: Proof. Firstly, we restate the optimization problem in Definition cite73†4.7 :\nL399:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}(K)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\max_{a}\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\mathbb{I}\\bigg[\\tau_{i}\\leq\\sum_{j=1}^{M}\\psi_{j}^{(i)}\\bigg]\\quad\\text{s.t.}$  |\nL400:  | $\\displaystyle\\psi_{j}^{(i)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{I}\\{a_{j}\\geq R_{j}(x^{(i)})\\}\\left(1+\\mathbb{I}\\{f_{j}(x^{(i)})\\in C_{s^{*}}^{(i)}\\}\\right),$  |\nL401:  | $\\displaystyle\\tau_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=(\\mu-v_{t^{(i)}})+\\sum_{b=1}^{s^{*}}(v_{b}-\\mu)$  |\nL402:  | $\\displaystyle\\quad\\mu$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi[s^{*}-1]+\\dfrac{(V_{c}[s^{*}]-\\Phi[s^{*}-1]+1)\\cdot s^{*}}{s^{*}+1},$  |\nL403:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^{T}a_{j}$  | $\\displaystyle=K,\\quad$  |\nL404:  | $\\displaystyle R_{j}(x^{(i)})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}K+1&\\text{if }f_{j}(x^{(i)})\\neq t^{(i)}\\\\\nL405: 1&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\\>,\\forall j$  |\nL406: We then note that $\\mu$ is the exact meeting point between the votes of the top $s^{*}$ classes that need to be reduced in order to elicit the prediction of $t^{(i)}$, and the initial votes for the target $v_{t^{(i)}}$. The gap or aggregation margin $\\tau_{i}$ can be then formalized as the sum off the differences between $\\mu$ and the $s^{*}+1$ classes in play.\nL407: From here on, the proof follows directly using the proof of Theorem cite113†A.1 , with two exceptions: (i) the most aggressive margin reduction occurs when an adversary flips predictions from the safe set $\\mathcal{C}_{s^{*}}$ to the harmful target $t^{(i)}$, rather than just from the top class (as was the case with DPA), and (ii) the shards that cannot be exploited are now those who already vote for the target $t^{(i)}$ (otherwise a vote would just be “wasted”). ∎\nL408: ## Appendix C Alignment Hyperparameters\nL409: \nL410: We align the pre-trained base models using Direct Policy Optimization (DPO) (cite39†Rafailov et al., 2023 ). To facilitate training within a constrained computational budget of two NVIDIA L40 (48GB) GPUs, we employ Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) via Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) (cite79†Hu et al., 2022 ) and weight quantization.\nL411: #### Model-Specific Configurations\nL412: \nL413: While many hyperparameters are shared to ensure experimental consistency, we optimize the learning rate, number of bits used for quantization, and LoRA scaling ($\\alpha$) for each specific architecture. These configurations are summarized in Table cite114†4 . We utilize the AdamW optimizer with a cosine learning rate scheduler and a linear warmup of $100$ steps.\nL414: Table 4: DPO Alignment Hyperparameters across models. If we denote the number of GPUs as $n_{g}$, the gradient accumultation steps as $g_{s}$ and the batch size per device as $b_{d}$, then the effective batch size is calculated as $b_{d}\\times g_{s}\\times n_{g}$.\nL415: Hyperparameter  | OLMo-1B  | Gemma2-2B  | Qwen1.5-4B\nL416: Quantization  | 8-bit  | 8-bit  | 4-bit\nL417: Learning Rate ($\\eta$)  | $1\\times 10^{-5}$  | $5\\times 10^{-5}$  | $5\\times 10^{-5}$\nL418: Effective Batch Size  | 80  | 80  | 64\nL419: Max Grad Norm  | 1.5  | 1.0  | 1.0\nL420: LoRA Rank ($r$)  | 64  | 64  | 64\nL421: LoRA Alpha ($\\alpha$)  | 32  | 128  | 64\nL422: Shared Hyperparameters\nL423: ---\nL424: DPO $\\beta$  | 0.1\nL425: Weight Decay  | 0.005\nL426: Optimizer  | AdamW\nL427: LR Scheduler  | Cosine\nL428: Warmup Steps  | 100\nL429: #### Training Budget and Partitioning\nL430: \nL431: To ensure a fair comparison between the single base model and the ensemble variants, we maintain a constant total number of gradient steps across both setups.\nL432: \nL433:   * •\nL434: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSample-Efficient Real-World Dexterous Policy Fine-Tuning via Action-Chunked Critics and Normalizing Flows (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.09580v1)\nciteturn31705view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31698view1\",\"lineno\":272}); Total lines: 710\nL194: Inspired by recent findings on value learning stability [cite114†15 , cite115†46 ], we parameterize the critic as a categorical distribution trained using a cross-entropy objective. We employ HL-Gauss [cite116†22 ] as the distributional parameterization, which improves stability and scalability in practice.\nL195: \nL196: Algorithm 1 Normalizing-flow policy over action chunks\nL197: \nL198: Input: Observation $o$, base distribution $p_{0}(z)$, invertible flow $f_{\\theta}(\\cdot;\\,o)$\nL199: \nL200: $c\\leftarrow\\mathrm{Enc}(o)$\nL201: Forward pass (likelihood evaluation):\nL202: \nL203: $z_{0}\\leftarrow f_{\\theta}(a;\\,c)$ $\\triangleright$ actions $\\rightarrow$ latent\nL204: \nL205: $\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a\\mid o)\\leftarrow\\log p_{0}(z_{0})+\\log\\left|\\det J_{f_{\\theta}}(a;\\,c)\\right|$\nL206: \nL207: Inverse pass (action generation):\nL208: \nL209: $z_{0}\\sim p_{0}(z)$\nL210: \nL211: $a\\leftarrow f_{\\theta}^{-1}(z_{0};\\,c)$ $\\triangleright$ latent $\\rightarrow$ actions\nL212: \nL213: $\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a\\mid o)\\leftarrow\\log p_{0}(z_{0})-\\log\\left|\\det J_{f_{\\theta}^{-1}}(z_{0};\\,c)\\right|$\nL214: Algorithm 2 Action-chunk selection with critic evaluation\nL215: \nL216: Input: Observation $o_{k}$, policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$, critic $Q_{\\phi}$, number of samples $N_{\\pi}$\nL217: \nL218: Output: Selected action chunk $a_{k}$\nL219: \nL220: $\\triangleright$ Sample and score candidate chunks\nL221: \nL222: for $i=1$ to $N_{\\pi}$ do\nL223: \nL224:    $a^{(i)}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid o_{k})$\nL225: \nL226:    $q^{(i)}\\leftarrow\\min_{j}Q_{\\phi}^{(j)}(o_{k},a^{(i)})$\nL227: \nL228: $a_{k}\\leftarrow\\arg\\max_{i}q^{(i)}$\nL229: \nL230: return $a_{k}$\nL231: \nL232: Algorithm 3 SOFT-FLOW training pipeline\nL233: Input: Demo data $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{demo}}$, offline data $\\mathcal{D}$\nL234: \nL235: Output: Fine-tuned policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$ and critic $Q_{\\phi}$\nL236: \nL237: $\\triangleright$ Stage I: Imitation learning\nL238: \nL239: foreach minibatch $(o,a)\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{demo}}$ do\nL240: \nL241:    $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IL}}\\leftarrow-\\mathbb{E}[\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a\\mid o)]$\nL242: \nL243:    $\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta_{\\pi}\\nabla_{\\theta}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IL}}$\nL244: \nL245: $\\triangleright$ Stage II: Offline critic warm-up\nL246: \nL247: repeat\nL248:    $\\hat{a}_{k+1}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}(\\cdot\\mid o_{k+1})$\nL249: \nL250:    $y\\leftarrow\\sum_{t=0}^{H-1}\\gamma^{t}r_{k,t}+\\gamma^{H}(1-d)Q_{\\bar{\\phi}}(o_{k+1},\\hat{a}_{k+1})$\nL251: \nL252:    $\\mathcal{L}_{Q}\\leftarrow\\text{CrossEntropy}(Q_{\\phi}(o_{k},a_{k}),y)$\nL253: \nL254:    $\\phi\\leftarrow\\phi-\\eta_{Q}\\nabla_{\\phi}\\mathcal{L}_{Q}$\nL255: \nL256: until convergence on $\\mathcal{D}$\nL257: \nL258: $\\triangleright$ Stage III: Full offline RL\nL259: \nL260: repeat\nL261: \nL262:    Update critic as above\nL263: \nL264:    $a_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\leftarrow$ Alg. cite117†2 L265:    Compute $\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{d}\\mid o)$ using Alg. cite113†1 L266: \nL267:    $\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta_{\\pi}(\\mathbb{E}[Q_{\\phi}(o,a_{\\pi_{\\theta}})]-\\lambda\\,\\mathbb{E}[\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{d}\\mid o)])$\nL268: \nL269: until convergence on $\\mathcal{D}$\nL270: \nL271: $\\triangleright$ Stage IV: Online fine-tuning\nL272: \nL273: while interaction budget remains do\nL274: \nL275:    Collect rollouts using Alg. cite117†2 L276: \nL277:    Add data to replay buffer\nL278: \nL279:    Update actor and critic with mixed (with ratio $\\rho$) offline and online data\nL280: ### IV-D Algorithm\nL281: \nL282: Our approach, S ample-Efficient O ff-Policy F ine-T uning via Action-Chunked Critics and Normalizing Flow s (SOFT-FLOW), consists of four training stages (Alg. cite118†3 ).\nL283: ##### I. Policy Initialization\nL284: \nL285: We first train the normalizing-flow policy using imitation learning on a filtered dataset of successful trajectories $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{demo}}$, a standard and effective baseline for robot policy learning:\nL286: \nL287:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{IL}}=\\mathbb{E}_{(o_{t},\\mathbf{a}^{*}_{t:t+H})\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{demo}}}\\Big[-\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{a}^{*}_{t:t+H}\\mid o_{t})\\Big]$$  |  | (8)\nL288: The resulting policy is denoted $\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}$. At inference time, we can sample multiple candidate action chunks from $\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}$, evaluate their likelihoods exactly, and select the most likely one. This strategy, which is difficult to implement for diffusion-based policies, has been shown to improve performance [cite91†29 ] and is also effective in our experiments.\nL289: ##### II. Offline RL critic warm-up\nL290: \nL291: Although imitation learning yields strong initial policies, it typically relies on high-quality demonstrations and cannot fully exploit suboptimal data. Reinforcement learning, by contrast, can leverage broader state–action coverage and optimize temporal efficiency through discounting. Because the critic is randomly initialized, we first warm up the critic by learning $Q$-values under $\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}$ before updating the policy. The target value is\nL292:  | $$\\sum_{t=0}^{H-1}\\gamma^{t}r_{k,t}+\\gamma^{H}(1-d)Q_{\\bar{\\phi}}(o_{k+1},\\hat{a}_{k+1}),\\quad\\hat{a}_{k+1}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}.$$  |\nL293: \nL294: This warm-started critic can already be used to improve online behavior by sampling multiple actions from $\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}$ and selecting the one with the highest estimated value, see Alg. cite117†2 .\nL295: ##### III. Full offline RL\nL296: \nL297: We then fine-tune $\\pi_{\\theta}$ using a full offline actor–critic pipeline. Directly maximizing $Q(o,a)$ can lead to exploitation of value estimation errors [cite104†27 ], so we regularize the actor toward the data distribution, following TD3+BC [cite119†17 ]:\nL298: \nL299:  | $$\\max_{\\theta}\\mathbb{E}[Q(o,a)]-\\lambda\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IL}}.$$  |\nL300: This simple formulation with appropriate design choices has been shown to outperform more complex alternatives [cite120†47 , cite121†48 , cite90†18 ] and adapts naturally to offline-to-online fine-tuning by reducing $\\lambda$ during online interaction [cite122†4 ].\nL301: ##### IV. Online RL fine-tuning\nL302: \nL303: Finally, we perform online fine-tuning on the real robot. Following cite99†Ball et al. [3] , we mix offline data with collected replay data at a fixed ratio $\\rho$ and apply imitation regularization only to the offline data. All other training components remain unchanged.\nL304: \nL305: ### IV-E Implementation Details\nL306: \nL307: Most hyperparameters are selected using simulation experiments (cite29†subsection V-A ) and transferred to real-world tasks without modification, unless otherwise noted.\nL308: ##### Policy architecture and normalizing flow\nL309: The policy is parameterized as a conditional normalizing flow with 16 coupling attention-based blocks, each with hidden dimensionality 256. We use an action chunk length of $H=10$ across all experiments. During imitation learning, ground-truth actions are perturbed with Gaussian noise $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_{\\text{noise}}^{2})$, with $\\sigma_{\\text{noise}}=0.05$ for simulation and $\\sigma_{\\text{noise}}=0.01$ for real-world tasks; this magnitude is consistent with prior work [cite92†49 ].\nL310: At inference time, we sample latent variables with standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\text{sample}}=0.7$, which provides a good trade-off between action diversity and distribution support.\nL311: ##### Reinforcement learning and critic configuration\nL312: \nL313: For offline and online RL, we use two critics, following standard practice. The imitation regularization coefficient is fixed to $\\lambda=0.1$ across all tasks. Distributional critic hyperparameters (including HL-Gauss configuration) are taken directly from cite115†Tarasov et al. [46] . Additional training parameters can be found in Appendix cite69†A-C2 .\nL314: ## V Experiments\nL315: \nL316: TABLE I: Success rates (over 4 seeds) on RoboMimic MH datasets.\nL317: Environment  | Imitation Learning  | Offline RL\nL318: --- | --- | ---\nL319: Lift  | $0.79\\pm 0.00$  | $0.91\\pm 0.04$\nL320: Can  | $0.96\\pm 0.00$  | $0.96\\pm 0.02$\nL321: Square  | $0.61\\pm 0.04$  | $0.68\\pm 0.05$\nL322: ### V-A Simulation Tasks\nL323: We first validate our approach in simulation to identify reasonable hyperparameters before deploying on real hardware. We use the RoboMimic benchmark [cite123†32 ] and consider the Lift, Can, and Square manipulation tasks. For all tasks, we use the Mixed Human (MH) datasets, which contain a mixture of successful and suboptimal demonstrations and are therefore more challenging than Expert Human datasets.\nL324: Policies operate on image-based observations with resolution $84\\times 84$, encoded using a ResNet-18 backbone [cite124†20 ]. We evaluate performance using success rate over 100 rollouts.\n"
